    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), Abschnitt »Zeugniße«" ;
    dcterms:source "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), pp. 6-7" .

pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicZeugnisJunckenTochter a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "John Juncken's daughter and son's wife, Nackenmixen, 1819"@en, "Johann Junckens Tochter und Sohnesfrau, Nackenmixen, 1819"@de ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:EuropeanFolkMagicGebrauchen ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicKopfwehStillung ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic ;
    skos:note "Both had violent pain in the head, and through the author it went. The family lives at Nackenmixen, near the Heckack, Bucks County; the year is eighteen nineteen."@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), Abschnitt »Zeugniße«" ;
    dcterms:source "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), pp. 6-7" .

pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicZeugnisJunckenBacken a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "The erysipelas on John Juncken's son's wife's cheek, 1819"@en, "Der Rothlauf am Backen von Junckens Sohnesfrau, 1819"@de ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:EuropeanFolkMagicGebrauchen ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicRothlaufStillung ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic ;
    skos:note "Beside the headache she had a sore cheek with an astonishing erysipelas on it, for which she had already lain some days in bed. The erysipelas went in some seven or nine hours; the cheek broke open and healed strongly."@en ;
    skos:note "The shortest interval named anywhere in the section, and the only one given as a range. Seven or nine hours is a measurement of the sort that can be missed, and it is the reason this case is separated from the headache it is reported with."@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), Abschnitt »Zeugniße«" ;
    dcterms:source "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), p. 7" .

pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicZeugnisArnold a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Arnold's daughter near Lebanon, 1815"@en, "Arnolds Tochter bei Libanon, 1815"@de ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:EuropeanFolkMagicGebrauchen ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicBrandNehmen ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic ;
    skos:note "The handle broke off the pot while she was pouring, the hot coffee ran over her arm and burnt it rather badly. The author states that he was present and saw it; he took the burn out, the arm did not go sore and healed in a very short time. The master Arnold lives not far from Lebanon, Lebanon County; the year is eighteen fifteen."@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), Abschnitt »Zeugniße«" ;
    dcterms:source "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), p. 7" .

pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicZeugnisStaufer a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Jacob Staufer's child at the Heckack, Bucks County"@en, "Jacob Staufers Kind am Heckack, Bucks Caunty"@de ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:EuropeanFolkMagicGebrauchen ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicGichterHeilung ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic ;
    skos:note "A small child that had the fits every hour. The author sold Staufer a book containing the twenty-five letters; on the urging of his neighbour Henrich Franckenfeld, Staufer used the twenty-five letters, and the child was at once freed of the fits and became well. The same letters, the author adds, are in this book too."@en ;
    skos:note "The only case in the section that names the means, the only one performed by the sufferer's own household rather than by the author, and the only one with a second named person as the mover. It is therefore the strongest of the fifteen for the transmission's own claim, since the book worked in a hand that was not its author's."@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), Abschnitt »Zeugniße«" ;
    dcterms:source "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), pp. 7-8" .

pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicZeugnisAlgaier a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "John Algaier of Reading, 1819"@en, "John Algaier in Reading, 1819"@de ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:EuropeanFolkMagicGebrauchen ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicRothlaufStillung ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic ;
    skos:note "A very sore finger with erysipelas. The author used for the erysipelas and for the healing of the finger; by the next morning the erysipelas was gone, the finger hardly hurt any more and began to heal strongly. The year is eighteen nineteen."@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), Abschnitt »Zeugniße«" ;
    dcterms:source "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), p. 8" .

pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicZeugnisAufnahmeregel a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The witnesses may be compelled before a justice of the peace"@en, "Die Zeugen sind vor einen Friedensrichter zu nötigen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The rule of admission the section closes with: should any of the named witnesses, who received help through the author or through his wife and through God, call him a liar and say that they were not helped, when they themselves acknowledged to him that they were, then he will compel them to acknowledge it once more before a justice of the peace, if it is possible; and he believes for the greater part that this can be done."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the part of the section that makes it more than a list. A transmission that names a forum in which its own witnesses can be made to repeat or withdraw their testimony has stated a procedure with an outcome that could have gone otherwise. Nothing else in the corpus does so."@en ;
    skos:note "The clause is also a limit on the claim it defends. The author offers to compel the acknowledgement, not to produce the cure; what he says can be tested before the justice is what the witness said, and not what happened to the eye."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), Abschnitt »Zeugniße«" ;
    dcterms:source "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), p. 7" .

#
# The Anglo-Saxon side of the corpus, entered for the first time.
#
# The counter-search for the exclusion clause of pmc:EuropeanFolkMagic-
# FallendeKrankheitAusschluss was carried in the Anglo-Saxon leechdoms, which
# pm:principalCorpus already names through Lacnunga and Bald's Leechbook. The
# clause is not there. What is there, in the same disease and in the collection
# printed beside them, is a remedy whose efficacy is bound to two calendar
# conditions at once, and it is entered because it was read.
#

pmp:EuropeanFolkMagicAsterionBeeren a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Berries of asterion eaten at the waning moon in Virgo"@en, "Beeren des asterion, gegessen bei abnehmendem Mond in der Jungfrau"@de ;
    skos:definition "Taking the berries of the wort called asterion and giving them to be eaten when the moon is on the wane, and that when the course of the sun is in the constellation named the Virgin, which is the month called August; and hanging the same wort on the sufferer's neck."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic ;
    dcterms:source "Herbarium Apuleii Platonici, chapter LXI, in Oswald Cockayne, Leechdoms, Wortcunning and Starcraft of Early England, vol. I (Rolls Series 35, London 1864), Old English text and translation, p. 165" .

pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicAsterionYieldsHeilung a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "The berries in their month take away the falling sickness"@en, "Die Beeren in ihrem Monat nehmen die fallende Krankheit"@de ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:EuropeanFolkMagicAsterionBeeren ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicFallendeKrankheitHeilung ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic ;
    skos:note "Two calendar conditions on one remedy, and they are of different kinds: a phase of the moon, which recurs monthly, and a position of the sun, which the text itself resolves into a single month of the year. Together they leave a window of a few days in August. The prescription says nothing about what happens outside it, which is why the placing below is a prescribed order and not a stated necessity."@en ;
    dcterms:source "Herbarium Apuleii Platonici, chapter LXI, in Oswald Cockayne, Leechdoms, Wortcunning and Starcraft of Early England, vol. I (Rolls Series 35, London 1864), p. 165" .

pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicAsterionPresupposesFenster a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "The eating presupposes the waning moon and the sun in the Virgin"@en, "Das Essen setzt abnehmenden Mond und die Sonne in der Jungfrau voraus"@de ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicFallendeKrankheitHeilung ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:EuropeanFolkMagicAsterionBeeren ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic ;
    skos:note "The second calendar window this file now holds for the same disease. Hohman's slip against the falling sickness is hung on at the first Friday in the new light; the Anglo-Saxon berries are eaten at the waning moon in August. The two windows are incompatible with each other and neither text knows of the other, which is a finding about the shape of the prescriptions and not about the moon."@en ;
    dcterms:source "Herbarium Apuleii Platonici, chapter LXI, in Oswald Cockayne, Leechdoms, Wortcunning and Starcraft of Early England, vol. I (Rolls Series 35, London 1864), p. 165" .
